# Native proof over accepted controller history

This development profile joins the existing honest v3 archive circuit to the
actual accepted native history of `development-v3-attempt2`. It follows the
native generation-3 pointer through its four accepted seals, each with five
required receipt units. The original controller's stale unaccepted branch is
not included. Its separate ordinary trajectory is not pooled into this proof.

The producer and a fresh consumer use native Kit verified reads and the
existing full/delta integrity-admission code on separate exact archive clones.
They reconstruct receipts from the original immutable local effect evidence,
check source and parent bindings, and verify the complete required unit sets.
The original store and ledger remain unchanged. This reader does not rerun the
original behavioral checks: their truth still depends on the original trusted
local execution. This is an archive-consumer integration, not a theorem that
tests executed correctly or a production trust admission.

Each canonical event commits its accepted seal/parent, source and original
binding, complete receipt-CID map and complete verified pack digest. Its SHA256
digest is passed to the standard four-slot v3 circuit. The native relation uses
SHA256 leaves/parents and a scalar-reduced public root. The added consumer
therefore checks the externally expected full root bytes, count, accepted
pointer, chain, binary and verifying key before native verification. The
already retained scalar-alias limitation still applies to a raw proof alone.

`run-v1/freeze.json` pins the selected chain, original archive/profile/ledger,
reader/service and existing source-built backend/keys before the run. The
statement is frozen after native source admission and before honest proving.
Only the original standard v3 binary is executed. The stopped custom v2
investigation is not imported or resumed.

The single observation completes with one honest proof and one runtime native
pairing verification. A separate audit adds one fresh verification, as allowed
by the freeze. No model or new behavioral-scorer calls occur.

| Observed work | Seconds |
| --- | ---: |
| Producer native archive read | 0.325531 |
| Fresh consumer native archive read | 0.342170 |
| Honest proof generation | 12.617511 |
| Runtime native verification | 0.006725 |
| Full measured driver | 14.749495 |
| Additional audit verification | 0.006085 |

The measured driver includes source/key/archive hashing, clones, reads, proof,
verification and mutation controls. The listed runtime phases are nested in
that driver interval and are not added again. Earlier backend compilation,
the 7.810198-second original key setup, historical controller work and the later
audit are outside that interval. This is one shared-host observation with
existing development keys. It establishes no direct-hashing speed advantage,
execution reuse, provider-token savings or amortized deployment benefit.

The proof JSON is 1,484 bytes. The source archive has 69 files / 2,010,722 bytes;
this includes other retained store records, whereas the selected proof covers
only its four accepted native events. Small proof bytes do not replace the
source/receipt custody the consumer still reads. The 154,403,632-byte proving
key and 648-byte verifying key are existing separately pinned setup artifacts.

All seven prospectively listed mutation controls reject: missing or corrupt
receipt bytes, incomplete leaf inventory, stale parent, reordered events,
changed event source commitment and changed full root. The first four execute
the actual native reader and preserve their specific rejection errors; the last
three are rejected by exact expected-statement binding before pairing. These
are bounded controls, not a general soundness rate.

`validate.py` recomputes event digests and the complete four-leaf Merkle root,
checks native read results/source imports and exact keys, checks specific
mutation failures, verifies unchanged original state and performs the second
actual pairing check. Its 218 checks pass; 34 observed source files are copied
to `run-v1/audited_source_archive/`. Cryptographic verification uses the same
native backend, not an independently implemented verifier.

The execution and audit are retained in `run-v1/{freeze,statement_freeze,
statement,events,summary,audit}.json`, with native commands and full process
outputs in `invocations/` and `reads/`. The scripts intentionally refuse to
overwrite the completed run. Another measurement requires an unused output
directory and a new recorded freeze; the old run must remain unchanged.
